iven column \\(C_a\\).\n\nSuppose that \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains fewer than \\(n-2\\) vertical lines.  Then\nthere exist at least two columns, say \\(C_{a_1},C_{a_2}\\) with\n\\(a_1,a_2\\le n-2\\), that are not covered by a vertical line.  Each of these\ncolumns contains at least two points, and each of those points must be covered\nby a distinct non‑vertical line (otherwise a single non‑vertical line would\ncontain two points of the same column, contradicting the observation above).\nHence at least \\(2+2=4\\) non‑vertical lines are needed for those two columns\nalone.\n\nRepeating this argument, if the family omitted the vertical lines for\n\\(t\\) different columns with \\(t\\ge 1\\), it would require at least \\(2t\\)\nnon‑vertical lines.  Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\n2t\\le n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\) are \\(n-2\\) in number.  If any of them\nwere omitted, we would need at least \\(2\\) non‑vertical lines for each omitted\ncolumn, quickly exceeding the budget \\(n\\).  A direct counting shows that the\nonly way to stay within \\(n\\) lines is to include the vertical lines for all\ncolumns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\); that supplies exactly \\(n-2\\) lines.\n\nConsequently the family can contain at most two lines that are not vertical.\nEvery non‑vertical line has a slope different from \\(\\infty\\); it may have slope\n\\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), which are non‑sunny, but regardless it is **at most two**\nsuch lines.  Hence the number of sunny lines (which are necessarily\nnon‑vertical) is bounded by \\(2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemmas 1–3 give explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\) and prove that no\nconfiguration can have \\(k\\ge 3\\).  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of\nsunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###