the \\(n\\) translated lines at least\nthree are non‑sunny (the induction hypothesis guarantees at least three\nnon‑sunny lines because \\(k\\le n-3\\)). Remove any three of those non‑sunny\ntranslated lines; the remaining \\(n\\) lines together with the three border\nlines give exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nThe number of sunny lines in the final family is unchanged: it is still\n\\(k\\). Since \\(k\\) can be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), after the\nconstruction we have realized every integer\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le (n+1)-3 .\n\\]\n\nThus the inductive step is proved.\n\nBy induction, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Section 1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nSection 2 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) such a\nconfiguration exists. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis concludes the solution."
