 resulting sunny lines are distinct.\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines for the remaining interior points.*  \n   For the other \\(|I_n|-k\\) interior points we draw non\u2011sunny lines, e.g.\n   vertical lines \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2\\) or horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) with\n   \\(c\\ge 2\\). Again the lines are distinct because the points are distinct.\n\n   Altogether we have  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\;(\\text{border lines})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;( |I_n|-k )\\;(\\text{non\u2011sunny})\n   =3+|I_n| = n\n   \\]\n\n   lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and they cover every point of\n   \\(T_n\\) (the border points are covered by the three border lines, the interior\n   points by the lines we attached to them).\n\n   Since the construction works for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\),\n   the bound (1) is also sufficient.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-3\\) and the explicit construction for every\n   \\(k\\) in that range give the complete description of the possible values of\n   \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\text{ are possible, and no other }k\\text{ works}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three\ndirections  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_v:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least three non\u2011sunny lines are necessary\n\nConsider the three subsets of \\(T_n\\)\n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nV_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nD_n=\\{(1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThey lie respectively on the three forbidden lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\n*Lemma.* A sunny line meets each of the sets \\(H_n,V_n,D_n\\) in at most one\npoint.\n\n*Proof.* If a line contains two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) then those points\nhave the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so the line is horizontal, i.e. parallel to\n\\(\\ell_h\\), contradicting sunniness. The same argument applies to \\(V_n\\) and\n\\(D_n\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently, to cover all points of \\(H_n\\) we must have the horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) among our collection; similarly we must have the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=2\\). These three lines are not sunny.\nHence any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least three\nnon\u2011sunny lines, and therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nB_n = H_n\\cup V_n\\cup D_n\n\\qquad\\text{(the \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 points)},\n\\]\n\\[\nI_n = T_n\\setminus B_n\n      =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n      \\qquad\\text{(the interior points)} .\n\\]\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(B_n\\).  \nThe interior \\(I_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\ge n-3\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints (a simple count of the integer lattice points in the triangle with\nvertices \\((2,2),(n-1,2),(2,n-1)\\)).\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 sunny lines.*  \nChoose arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(I_n\\).\nFor each \\(P_i\\) draw a line of a sunny direction passing through \\(P_i\\);\nfor definiteness we may take the line of slope \\(2\\) (i.e. direction not\nparallel to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). Because the points are distinct, the resulting\n\\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct.\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines for the remaining interior points.*  \nFor every remaining point \\(Q\\in I_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\\) draw a\nnon\u2011sunny line that passes through \\(Q\\). Two convenient choices are:\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) if \\(c\\ge 2\\) (parallel to \\(\\ell_v\\)), or\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) if \\(c\\ge 2\\) (parallel to \\(\\ell_h\\)).\n\nSince each such line is determined by its unique interior point, all these\nlines are distinct and non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 total collection.*  \nCollect the three border lines (2) together with the lines constructed in\nSteps\u202f1 and\u202f2. The total number of lines is\n\n\\[\n3\\;+\\;k\\;+\\;\\bigl(|I_n|-k\\bigr)=3+|I_n|=n,\n\\]\n\nusing (3). Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (those from Step\u202f1). By construction\nevery point of \\(B_n\\) lies on one of the three border lines, and every point\nof \\(I_n\\) lies on the line that was drawn through it in Step\u202f1 or\u202f2. Hence all\npoints of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the required covering property and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe argument of Section\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\le n-3\\). Section\u202f2 exhibits, for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), a configuration that meets the two conditions and has\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Therefore the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis finishes the problem."
