cap T_n=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad d_3\\cap T_n=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n1. **Two fixed non‑sunny diagonals.**  \n   Keep  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:d_2\\;(x+y=2),\\qquad \\ell_2:d_3\\;(x+y=3).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non‑sunny and cover the points of \\(d_2\\) and \\(d_3\\).\n\n2. **Select \\(k\\) further non‑sunny diagonals.**  \n   Choose a subset \\(\\mathcal K\\subset\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with\n   \\(|\\mathcal K|=k\\).  For each \\(c\\in\\mathcal K\\) keep the whole diagonal\n   \\(d_c\\) as a line (non‑sunny).\n\n3. **Sunny lines for the remaining diagonals.**  \n   For every \\(c\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\mathcal K\\) (there are\n   \\((n-2)-k\\) such indices) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_c:\\; y=x+c-2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\sigma_c\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from the three\n   forbidden slopes; hence each \\(\\sigma_c\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Coverage of a diagonal \\(d_c\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in d_c\\); then \\(a+b=c\\).  Substituting \\(b=c-a\\) into the\n   equation of \\(\\sigma_c\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   c-a = a + c-2 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; 2a=2 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; a=1,\n   \\]\n\n   whence \\(b=c-1\\).  Thus the only point of \\(d_c\\) that lies on\n   \\(\\sigma_c\\) is \\((1,c-1)\\).  Consequently:\n\n   * if \\(c\\in\\mathcal K\\) the whole diagonal \\(d_c\\) is present as a line,\n     so every point of \\(d_c\\) is covered;\n   * if \\(c\\notin\\mathcal K\\) the point \\((1,c-1)\\) is covered by\n     \\(\\sigma_c\\), while every *other* point of the same diagonal belongs to a\n     diagonal with a larger index (because its \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(1\\)).\n     That larger diagonal is either kept (if its index lies in \\(\\mathcal K\\))\n     or has its own sunny line \\(\\sigma_{c'}\\) that covers its first point.\n     Hence, by induction on the index, every point of \\(d_c\\) is eventually\n     covered.\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The family consists of  \n\n   * the two diagonals \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\);  \n   * the \\(k\\) diagonals \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathcal K\\);  \n   * the \\((n-2)-k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_c\\).\n\n   Their total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   2+k+\\bigl((n-2)-k\\bigr)=n .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   Exactly the \\((n-2)-k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_c\\) are sunny; the remaining\n   \\(2+k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  Hence the construction contains precisely\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\nall those values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nLemma 1 shows that at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. at most\n\\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.  The construction above shows that this bound is\ntight for every admissible \\(k\\).  Therefore the set of possible numbers of\nsunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
