longing to \\(S_n\\).  \n   Moreover, for distinct values of \\(t\\) the lines \\(S_t\\) are distinct, and none of them coincides with \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The set \\(\\{S_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n\\}\\) contains \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, each covering two new points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The two vertical lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) together cover all remaining points of \\(S_n\\) (those with \\(x\\ge 3\\) are already hit by some \\(S_t\\)).  \n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_k=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{S_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le k+2\\}, & 0\\le k\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\text{(any superset of the above for larger }k),\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the first \\(k\\) members of the \\(S_t\\) family) together with the two non‑sunny lines, giving a total of \\(k+2\\) lines.  \n   If \\(k<n-2\\) we simply omit the remaining \\(S_t\\)’s; the uncovered points are none, because the omitted sunny lines would have covered points already covered by \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).  \n   Finally, to reach the required total of \\(n\\) lines we add any \\(n-(k+2)\\) further non‑sunny lines (for instance additional vertical lines \\(x=3,4,\\dots\\) that are already present in the set of points, or horizontal lines \\(y=1,2\\)). These extra lines are non‑sunny and do not affect the count of sunny lines.\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) together with the explicit construction for each admissible \\(k\\) shows that the set of feasible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) form a right‑angled triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\); its cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a **non‑sunny** line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}: y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}: x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}: x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a non‑sunny line has an equation of one of the forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c \\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}(\\ell_i\\cap S_n). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.** *A sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most two points.*  \n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be sunny, with equation \\(y=mx+b\\) where \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b')\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) then \\(b'=ma+b\\) for some integer \\(b\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b')\\) satisfies \\(a+b'\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na+ma+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq -1\\), the right‑hand side is a real number; at most two integer values of \\(a\\) can satisfy the inequality, and each determines a unique \\(b'\\). Hence \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le 2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.** *A non‑sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.*  \n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three families in (2).\n\n* If \\(\\ell:x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.\n* If \\(\\ell:y=c\\) the same count holds.\n* If \\(\\ell:x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then the points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them.\n\nThus in every case \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\nNow count the total number of incidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and the points of \\(S_n\\).  \nUsing Lemma 1 for the \\(k\\) sunny lines and Lemma 2 for the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl|\\ell_i\\cap S_n\\bigr|\n   \\le 2k + (n-k)\\,n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the covering condition (3) the left‑hand side of (4) must be at least \\(|S_n|\\) given by (1). Hence\n\n\\[\n2k + (n-k)n \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n2k + n^{2} - kn \\ge \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk(2-n) \\ge -\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(2-n<0\\); dividing by this negative number reverses the inequality and yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that the bound (5) is sharp.\n\n##### 3.1  Two fixed non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=2 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (vertical).  \n\\(L_1\\) contains the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);  \n\\(L_2\\) contains the points \\((2,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(1\\) or \\(2\\) is already covered.\n\n##### 3.2  A family of sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(3\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = -(t-2)\\,x + (t+1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-(t-2)\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that \\(S_t\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t-2,\\,3),\\qquad Q_t=(t-1,\\,2),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(S_n\\) because \\(t-2\\ge 1\\), \\(t-1\\ge 2\\) and  \n\n\\[\n(t-2)+3 = t+1\\le n+1,\\qquad (t-1)+2 = t+1\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nNo other point of \\(S_n\\) lies on \\(S_t\\); indeed Lemma 1 guarantees at most two such points, and we have exhibited two.  \nFor distinct values of \\(t\\) the lines \\(S_t\\) are distinct, and none coincides with \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\{S_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, each covering a pair of points of \\(S_n\\) that is not covered by the other members of \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n##### 3.3  Completing the family to \\(n\\) lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSelect the first \\(k\\) sunny lines from \\(\\mathcal S\\); denote this subfamily by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).  \nConsider the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k = \\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\mathcal S_k .\n\\]\n\nIt contains \\(k+2\\) distinct lines.  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are covered:\n\n* Points with \\(x=1\\) or \\(x=2\\) are covered by \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n* For each \\(t\\) with \\(3\\le t\\le k+2\\) the two points \\(P_t,Q_t\\) are covered by \\(S_t\\).\n* Any remaining point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\) and \\(b\\ge 3\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); then \\(a+b = t+1\\) for some \\(t\\) in the range \\(3\\le t\\le n\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) equals either \\(P_t\\) or \\(Q_t\\) for that \\(t\\), and therefore is covered by the corresponding \\(S_t\\). If \\(t>k+2\\) the point is already covered by \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\) because its \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(2\\); otherwise it is covered by a line in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) already satisfies the covering condition.  \nIf \\(k+2<n\\) we add any \\(n-(k+2)\\) further non‑sunny lines (for instance the vertical lines \\(x=3,4,\\dots\\) or horizontal lines \\(y=1,2\\)). These extra lines do not change the number of sunny lines and keep all lines distinct.\n\nConsequently for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling the required covering property and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the necessary inequality (5) with the explicit construction, we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\nThat is, any integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can occur, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible. ∎"
