1,\\dots ,n-1\\) together with the sunny line), which exceeds the allowed number \\(n\\).  Hence even the special column \\(C_n\\) cannot be used to obtain a configuration with a sunny line while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n   Therefore any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all required points must consist solely of lines parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. **no line can be sunny**.  Hence \\(k\\) must be \\(0\\).\n\nCombining the construction for \\(k=0\\) with the impossibility proof for \\(k\\ge 1\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nDefine the set of lattice points that must be covered:\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) set\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal P\\) is the disjoint union of the \u201ccolumns\u2019\u2019 \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n\\[\nL_a:\\;x=a\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\nEach \\(L_a\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so by definition it is **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nHence the family \\(\\{L_a\\}_{a=1}^{n}\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nThe lines are distinct and the number of sunny lines among them is \\(k=0\\).\n\nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No sunny line can appear in a feasible configuration\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line.  \nLet \\(S\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a sunny line.