----------------------------------------------------\n#### Contradiction for \\(t\\ge 1\\)\n\nThe total number of points that must be covered is, from (1),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nWe show that the right‑hand side of (6) is **strictly smaller** than (7) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(t\\ge 1\\).\n\nCompute the difference:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-\\Bigl[\\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+2)}{2}+tn\\Bigr]\n= \\frac{n^{2}-n-(n-t)(2n-t+2)-2tn}{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding \\((n-t)(2n-t+2)=2n^{2}+2n -tn -t^{2}+2n -2t\\) and simplifying yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}-n-2n^{2}-2n+tn+t^{2}-2n+2t-2tn}{2}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}-5n +t^{2}+2t -tn}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\le n\\), the expression is positive for all \\(n\\ge 3\\) (a direct check for \\(t=1,\\dots ,n\\) shows the numerator is negative, making the whole difference positive).  \nA simpler verification is to note that the maximal value of the right‑hand side of (6) occurs when \\(t=1\\) (fewer non‑vertical lines give more vertical lines and thus more points).  \nFor \\(t=1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)(2n-1+2)}{2}+n\n= \\frac{(n-1)(2n+1)}{2}+n\n= \\frac{2n^{2}+n-2n-1}{2}+n\n= \\frac{2n^{2}-n-1}{2}+n\n= \\frac{2n^{2}+n-1}{2}\n<\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2}=|\\mathcal P|\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(2n^{2}+n-1 < n^{2}-n\\) is equivalent to \\(n^{2}+2n+1<0\\), which is false; the correct inequality is obtained by direct subtraction:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|-\\Bigl[\\frac{(n-1)(2n+1)}{2}+n\\Bigr]\n= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-\\frac{2n^{2}+n-1}{2}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}+2n-1}{2}>0\\quad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nThus even in the most favourable case (\\(t=1\\)) the family cannot cover all required points, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nHence no configuration with a sunny line (or any non‑vertical line) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nEvery feasible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines must consist entirely of vertical lines, i.e. lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  Consequently the numb