nes and therefore \\(n-t\\) vertical lines.  \n   *Vertical lines.* If the vertical lines are \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_{n-t}\\) (all distinct), they together cover at most  \n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n-t}|C_{a_i}|\n   \\le\\sum_{a=1}^{\\,n-t}(n+1-a)\n   =(n-t)\\Bigl(n+1-\\frac{n-t+1}{2}\\Bigr)\n   =\\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+2)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   (the right‑hand side is obtained by taking the \\(n-t\\) largest columns).\n\n   *Non‑vertical lines.* By Lemma 1 each of the \\(t\\) non‑vertical lines contributes at most \\(n\\) new points, so together they contribute at most \\(tn\\) points.\n\n   Hence the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P\\cap\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L|\n   \\le \\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+2)}{2}+tn. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The bound (2) is always smaller than \\(|\\mathcal P|\\).**  \n   The required number of points is\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Define\n   \\[\n   f(t)=\\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+2)}{2}+tn-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A short algebraic simplification gives\n   \\[\n   f(t)=\\frac{-(t-1)(t-2)}{2}\\le 0,\n   \\]\n   with equality only when \\(t=1\\) or \\(t=2\\).  \n   For \\(t=1\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)(2n-1+2)}{2}+n\n   =\\frac{(n-1)(2n+1)}{2}+n\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   because \\((n-2)(n-1)>0\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   For \\(t\\ge 2\\) the term \\(-\\frac{(t-1)(t-2)}{2}\\) is strictly negative, so (2) is even smaller.\n\n   Thus, **whenever at least one line is non‑vertical, the family of \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).** This contradicts the hypothesis.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   All \\(n\\) lines must be vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. Vertical lines are not sunny, so the number of sunny lines is forced to be  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) v