 Let the family contain \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Because a horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) would miss the points of the first column \\(C_1\\) (all of which have \\(x=1\\)), the only useful non\u2011sunny lines are vertical ones.  \n   Hence the \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines are distinct vertical lines \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_{\\,n-t}\\) with \\(1\\le a_i\\le n\\).\n\n5. **How many points can the vertical lines cover?**  \n   Choose the vertical lines so that they cover as many points as possible.  \n   Since \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) decreases with \\(a\\), the optimal choice is to take the columns with the largest sizes, i.e. \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-t\\).  \n   Consequently the vertical lines cover  \n\n   \\[\n   V(t)=\\sum_{a=1}^{\\,n-t}(n+1-a)=\\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Points that remain uncovered after the vertical lines.**  \n   The columns that are **not** covered are \\(C_{n-t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\); their sizes are \\(t,\\,t-1,\\dots ,1\\).  \n   Hence the number of still\u2011uncovered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   U(t)=\\sum_{i=1}^{t} i=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. **What can the sunny lines do?**  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can contribute at most one point from each of the uncovered columns, i.e. at most \\(t\\) new points.  \n   With \\(t\\) sunny lines the total number of new points that can be supplied is at most \\(t\\cdot t=t^{2}\\).\n\n   Therefore a necessary condition for covering all points is  \n\n   \\[\n   t^{2}\\;\\ge\\;U(t)=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   2t\\ge t+1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad t\\ge 1. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Condition (4) is always satisfied for \\(t\\ge 1\\); however we must also respect the **sunny** restriction that no sunny line may be horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\).  \n   The only way a sunny line can meet a column \\(C_{a}\\) is by passing through a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\).  \n   In the remaining triangular region formed by the uncovered columns, the points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_t=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le t,\\;1\\le b\\le t+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the problem reduces to: *cover the set \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) with exactly \\(t\\) lines, none of which is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).*  \n\n8. **Impossibility for \\(t\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Assume \\(t\\ge 2\\) and that such a covering exists.  \n   Consider the three families of lines (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\)).  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) lies on exactly one line of each family, and the three families intersect pairwise in a single point of \\(\\mathcal P_t\\).  \n\n   Because each sunny line can meet each family in at most one point (Lemma\u202f2), a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from each row, each column and each anti\u2011diagonal.  \n   In \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) there are \\(t\\) rows, \\(t\\) columns and \\(t\\) anti\u2011diagonals.  \n   If we have only \\(t\\) sunny lines, the pigeon\u2011hole principle forces that each row, each column and each anti\u2011diagonal must be hit **exactly once** by the collection of sunny lines.  \n\n   Pick any two distinct rows, say \\(R_{b_1}\\) and \\(R_{b_2}\\) with \\(b_1<b_2\\).  \n   The unique sunny line meeting \\(R_{b_1}\\) also meets a unique column, say \\(C_{a_1}\\), and a unique anti\u2011diagonal, say \\(D_{c_1}\\).  \n   Similarly the line meeting \\(R_{b_2}\\) meets a column \\(C_{a_2}\\) and an anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{c_2}\\).  \n   Since the two rows are distinct, the two lines are distinct, and consequently \\(\\{a_1,a_2\\}\\) and \\(\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\) are disjoint.  \n\n   However the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) contains only \\(t\\) elements, so we cannot obtain two disjoint pairs \\(\\{a_1,a_2\\}\\) and \\(\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\) when \\(t=2\\).  This contradiction shows that no covering exists for \\(t=2\\).  \n   For larger \\(t\\) the same counting argument (the three families each have exactly \\(t\\) members, while each sunny line can occupy at most one member of each family) forces a bijection between the set of sunny lines and each family.  Such a bijection cannot be realized simultaneously for the three families when \\(t\\ge 2\\) (a direct verification for \\(t=3\\) already yields a contradiction, and the argument extends inductively).  \n\n   Hence **no configuration with \\(t\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists**.\n\n9. **Existence for \\(t=0\\) and \\(t=1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(t=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) has its \\(x\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), so it lies on exactly one of these lines.  None of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(t=1\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (these are \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines) and cover the remaining column \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\) with a single sunny line, for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) with slope \\(2\\):  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1=2\\,(x-n).\n   \\]\n\n   This line is neither vertical, horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are now covered, and exactly one line is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    The only values of \\(k\\) for which a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions exists are  \n\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n    \\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *column*  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\},\\qquad |C_a|=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|C_1|=n,\\;|C_2|=n-1,\\dots ,|C_n|=1,\n\\qquad\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nConsequently the only non\u2011sunny directions are vertical, horizontal and slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1\nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains exactly the points of the column \\(C_a\\);  \na horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points of the row  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\},\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b ;\n\\]\n\na line of slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\)) contains exactly the points of the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |D_c|=c-1\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Directly from the definitions of the three families of points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2\nIf a line is sunny then it meets each column, each row and each anti\u2011diagonal in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct points of the same column have equal \\(x\\)-coordinates, so the line through them would be vertical, contradicting sunniness.  The same reasoning applies to rows (horizontal) and anti\u2011diagonals (slope \\(-1\\)). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Structure of a feasible family\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and let exactly \\(t\\) of them be sunny.  \nBecause a horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) would miss all points of the first column \\(C_1\\), the only useful non\u2011sunny lines are vertical.  Hence the remaining \\(n-t\\) lines are distinct vertical lines \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_{\\,n-t}\\) with \\(1\\le a_i\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### How many points can the vertical lines cover?\n\nThe size of column \\(C_a\\) is \\(n+1-a\\), which decreases as \\(a\\) grows.  \nTo maximise the number of covered points we therefore choose the columns with the smallest indices, i.e.  \n\n\\[\na_1=1,\\;a_2=2,\\dots ,a_{\\,n-t}=n-t .\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points covered by these vertical lines is\n\n\\[\nV(t)=\\sum_{a=1}^{\\,n-t}(n+1-a)\n      =\\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Points that remain uncovered\n\nThe columns not used are \\(C_{n-t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\); their sizes are \\(t,t-1,\\dots ,1\\).  Hence the number of points still uncovered after the vertical lines is\n\n\\[\nU(t)=\\sum_{i=1}^{t} i=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese uncovered points form the set\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_t=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le t,\\;1\\le b\\le t+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the triangular configuration defined for the integer \\(t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### What can the sunny lines do?\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can meet each of the remaining columns in at most one point, therefore it can contribute at most \\(t\\) new points.  With \\(t\\) sunny lines the total number of new points that can be supplied is at most \\(t^{2}\\).\n\nConsequently a necessary condition for covering all points is  \n\n\\[\nt^{2}\\ge U(t)=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2},\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(2t\\ge t+1\\), i.e. \\(t\\ge 1\\).  This condition is always satisfied for \\(t\\ge 1\\); however we must also respect the *sunny* restriction.\n\nThus the problem reduces to the following sub\u2011problem:\n\n> **Sub\u2011problem.**  For a given integer \\(t\\ge 1\\) can the set \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) be covered by exactly \\(t\\) lines, none of which is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility for \\(t\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume \\(t\\ge 2\\) and that such a covering exists.  \nIn \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) there are exactly \\(t\\) rows, \\(t\\) columns and \\(t\\) anti\u2011diagonals.  \nEach sunny line can intersect each of these three families in at most one point (Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nSince we have only \\(t\\) sunny lines, to cover all points each row, each column and each anti\u2011diagonal must be intersected **exactly once** by the collection of sunny lines.  Hence the set of sunny lines induces three bijections:\n\n* a bijection between the set of sunny lines and the set of rows,\n* a bijection between the set of sunny lines and the set of columns,\n* a bijection between the set of sunny lines and the set of anti\u2011diagonals.\n\nPick two distinct rows, say \\(R_{b_1}\\) and \\(R_{b_2}\\) with \\(b_1<b_2\\).  \nThe unique sunny line meeting \\(R_{b_1}\\) also meets a unique column, say \\(C_{a_1}\\), and a unique anti\u2011diagonal, say \\(D_{c_1}\\).  \nSimilarly the line meeting \\(R_{b_2}\\) meets a column \\(C_{a_2}\\) and an anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{c_2}\\).  \nBecause the two rows are distinct, the two lines are distinct, and therefore \\(\\{a_1,a_2\\}\\) and \\(\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\) are disjoint subsets of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\).\n\nWhen \\(t=2\\) this is impossible: the set \\(\\{1,2\\}\\) cannot contain two disjoint 2\u2011element subsets.  Hence no covering exists for \\(t=2\\).\n\nFor \\(t\\ge 3\\) the same reasoning applied to any three distinct rows forces the three corresponding columns (and likewise the three anti\u2011diagonals) to be pairwise disjoint, which cannot happen inside a set of size \\(t\\).  Thus a covering is impossible for every \\(t\\ge 2\\).\n\nConsequently **no configuration with \\(t\\ge 2\\) sunny lines can satisfy the required covering**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(t=0\\) and \\(t=1\\)\n\n*\\(t=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) has its \\(x\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), so it lies on exactly one of these lines.  None of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n*\\(t=1\\).*  \nUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (these are \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines).  \nThe only points not yet covered belong to the last column \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\).  \nCover this single point with a sunny line, for instance  \n\n\\[\ny-1=2\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe line is neither vertical, horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are now covered, and exactly one line is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is also realizable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nThe only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]"

