ints covered elsewhere.  \n   Hence in any optimal configuration all non\u2011sunny lines may be taken to be vertical.\n\n5. **Let \\(t\\) be the number of sunny lines.**  \n   The remaining \\(n-t\\) lines are distinct vertical lines \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_{\\,n-t}\\) with \\(1\\le a_i\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Maximum points covered by the vertical lines.**  \n   Because \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) decreases with \\(a\\), the best choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-t).\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines then cover  \n\n   \\[\n   V(t)=\\sum_{a=1}^{\\,n-t}(n+1-a)=\\frac{(n-t)(2n-t+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Points that remain uncovered.**  \n   The uncovered columns are \\(C_{n-t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\); their sizes are \\(t,t-1,\\dots ,1\\).  \n   Hence the number of still\u2011uncovered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   U(t)=\\sum_{i=1}^{t} i=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   These points form the triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_t=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le t,\\;1\\le b\\le t+1-a\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n8. **What the \\(t\\) sunny lines can achieve.**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f3.*  \n   *A sunny line can contain at most one point from each column of \\(\\mathcal T_t\\); therefore it contains at most \\(t\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_t\\).*\n\n   Consequently the \\(t\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(t\\cdot t=t^{2}\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_t\\).  \n   Since \\(|\\mathcal T_t|=U(t)=\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\\), we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   t^{2}\\ge\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;t\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n   which is always satisfied for \\(t\\ge1\\).  Thus the counting argument alone does **not** rule out \\(t\\ge2\\); a sharper obstruction is needed.\n\n9. **A sharper obstruction (intersection counting).**  \n\n   Each sunny line meets each column at most once, so it contains at most \\(t\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_t\\).  \n   If a sunny line actually meets **every** column, it must contain exactly one point from each column; we call such 