as its \\(x\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), so it lies on exactly one of these lines.  None of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n    *\\(t=1\\) (i.e. \\(k=1\\)).*  \n    Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (these are \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines).  \n    The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  Cover it with a sunny line, for instance  \n\n    \\[\n    y-1=2\\,(x-n) \\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{)} .\n    \\]\n\n    This line is neither vertical, horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are now covered, and exactly one line is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is also attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n11. **Conclusion.**  \n    The only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions are  \n\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n    \\]"
