"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while no configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 show that at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny.  \n   *Every* point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on a line of one of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   By counting the points that can be covered by a single sunny line we prove that a single sunny line can miss at most one of the required points; consequently at least two lines must belong to the forbidden families. Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)** \u2013 give an explicit family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all required points.  \n   * Choose two vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   * For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) add a sunny line \\(L_i\\) of slope \\(2\\) passing through \\((i,1)\\).  \n   * Add the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all of slope \\(-1\\), each passing through a distinct point \\((1,j)\\) with \\(j=2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n\n   A straightforward verification shows that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nThe two parts together give the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a sunny line have slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nWrite it in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) (the case of a vertical line is excluded).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) to lie on this line we need  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+c\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad c=b-ma .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are positive integers, the right\u2013hand side can take at most\n\\(n\\) different integer values when \\((a,b)\\) ranges over \\(T_n\\):\nindeed \\(a\\) can be any integer from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), and for each fixed \\(a\\) the\npossible values of \\(b\\) are \\(1,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); thus the set\n\\(\\{b-ma\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n\\}\\) has cardinality at most \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently a single sunny line can contain **at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)**.\nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), at least two\ndifferent lines are needed to cover all points of \\(T_n\\) that are not\nalready covered by a sunny line.\n\nIf a line is not sunny, it is either horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)),\nvertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\nEach such line can contain arbitrarily many points of \\(T_n\\); in particular,\na vertical line \\(x=t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) contains all points \\((t,b)\\) with\n\\(b\\le n+1-t\\).\n\nHence, in any admissible configuration at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be\nnon\u2011sunny.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction uses three families of lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1 Two vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nV_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad V_2:\\;x=2 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny (vertical).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2 \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(2\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=2x-(2i-1) .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\); its slope \\(2\\) is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nAll the \\(S_i\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3 \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) that is **not** among the\nnumbers \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) (there are exactly \\(n-2-k\\) such \\(j\\)) define  \n\n\\[\nD_j:\\; x+y=j .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nThe lines \\(D_j\\) are distinct because they have different constants on the\nright\u2011hand side.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.4 Verification that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered  \n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\) or \\(a=2\\)*, the point lies on \\(V_1\\) or \\(V_2\\) respectively.\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 3\\)*, then \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nConsider the integer \\(s=a-b\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le s = a-b \\le a-1 \\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nTwo cases occur.\n\n1. **\\(s\\le k\\).**  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=2a-(2s-1)\\), i.e. it lies on the sunny line\n   \\(S_s\\).\n\n2. **\\(s>k\\).**  \n   Since \\(s\\) is an integer between \\(k+1\\) and \\(n-1\\), it is one of the\n   constants used for the lines \\(D_j\\).  Indeed \\(a+b = a+(a-s)=2a-s\\), so\n   \\(a+b = s\\) is equivalent to \\(x+y=s\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n   \\(D_s\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5 Counting sunny lines  \n\nThe only sunny lines in the construction are the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\nAll other lines (\\(V_1,V_2\\) and the \\(D_j\\)\u2019s) are non\u2011sunny.  Hence the total\nnumber of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\nthe set of attainable values of \\(k\\) contains this whole interval.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction of \u00a72 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed attainable.\n\nTherefore the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
