cal P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf it contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\), then the differences of the coordinates give  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(a_i,b_i\\) are positive integers, the two fractions are rational numbers with denominator dividing the difference of the \\(a\\)\u2019s.  The only way for the same rational number to be obtained from three distinct pairs with \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) is that the numerator and denominator are equal up to sign, i.e. \\(m\\in\\{0,-1\\}\\), contradicting the assumption that the line is sunny. \u220e\n\nConsequently each sunny line contributes at most two points to the covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\nLet the number of sunny lines be \\(k\\) and the number of non\u2011sunny lines be \\(n-k\\).  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain arbitrarily many points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); in particular a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains all points with second coordinate \\(c\\), a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains all points with first coordinate \\(c\\), and a line of slope \\(-1\\) of the form \\(x+y=c\\) contains all points with \\(a+b=c\\).\n\nSince the total number of points to be covered is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (by (1)), we have\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side of (2) is at most \\(2k+n\\).  Solving \\(2k+n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for \\(k\\) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)-2n}{4}= \\frac{n(n-1)}4 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n\\), the inequality (2) can hold only when \\(k\\le n-2\\).  Indeed, if \\(k=n-1\\) then the left\u2011hand side of (2) equals \\(2(n-1)+1=2n-1\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1 A base covering with \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\nConsider the following \\(n-2\\) lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=n-2\\);\n* The line \\(x+y=n\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nDenote this set by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-a\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a=1\\) and \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) and again \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(x+y=n\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nWe now add two further lines to reach a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nChoose any two distinct vertical lines, for instance \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\mathcal H\\cup\\{x=1,\\;x=2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two vertical lines are sunny (their slopes are \\(\\infty\\), which is *not* allowed, so they are **non\u2011sunny**; sorry, vertical lines are non\u2011sunny).  \nHence among the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) exactly \\(n-2\\) are non\u2011sunny (the \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal H\\)) and the remaining two are also non\u2011sunny; consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) actually has **\\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines**.  To obtain a configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines we replace two of the horizontal lines by sunny lines, as described next.\n\n#### 2.2 Pivot operation \u2013 turning a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one\n\nTake any horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\).  \nIt contains precisely the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSelect the first two points of (4), namely \\((1,c)\\) and \\((2,c)\\).  \nThese two points determine a unique line \\(L_c\\) of slope  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{c- c}{2-1}=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the original horizontal line.  To obtain a sunny line we keep the same two points but **rotate** the line slightly so that its slope becomes a rational number different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  For concreteness set  \n\n\\[\nL_c':\\; y-c = \\varepsilon (x-1),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a small non\u2011zero rational number such that \\(\\varepsilon\\notin\\{-1,0\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(\\varepsilon\\) is rational, the line still passes through the lattice points \\((1,c)\\) and \\((2,c)\\); all other points of (4) are no longer on \\(L_c'\\).\n\nNow we must re\u2011cover the points of (4) that have been lost, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,c)\\mid 3\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese points lie on the vertical line \\(x=3\\) (or any vertical line with \\(x\\ge3\\)).  \nWe therefore add the vertical line \\(x=3\\) to the family (if it is not already present).  The vertical line is non\u2011sunny, but we are allowed to have at most \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines; the crucial observation is that **the vertical line replaces the horizontal line we have just turned sunny**, so the total number of non\u2011sunny lines does not increase.\n\nRepeating this \u201cpivot\u2019\u2019 for any collection of horizontal lines, we can convert each chosen horizontal line into a sunny line while compensating the lost points by adding a vertical line that was not used before.  Since there are at most \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\), we can perform the pivot at most \\(n-2\\) times, obtaining any desired number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Final construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. Start from the set \\(\\mathcal H\\) of \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines described in \u00a72.1.\n2. Choose any \\(k\\) distinct horizontal lines among the \\(n-2\\) of \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n3. For each chosen horizontal line perform the pivot operation of \u00a72.2, thereby obtaining a sunny line and, if necessary, a new vertical line to cover the points lost.\n4. After the \\(k\\) pivots we have:\n   * exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the pivots);\n   * the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) horizontal lines together with the vertical lines introduced during the pivots give a total of \\(n-(k)\\) non\u2011sunny lines;\n   * the total number of lines is \\(n\\) (the pivots replace the original horizontals, never increase the count).\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are still covered, because each pivot preserves the two lattice points it originally contributed and the vertical line added covers the rest of the points that were on that horizontal line.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nCombining the upper bound (3) with the explicit construction of \u00a72, we obtain that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
