nsequently none of them is sunny.\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m=n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points already covered\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa at most \\(m\\) is already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The remaining region\n\nAssume now that \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a>m\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence all uncovered points belong to  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Sunny lines covering \\(R\\)\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in R\\) define the integer  \n\n\\[\nd=a-b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\in[m+1,n]\\) and \\(b\\in[1,k]\\),\n\n\\[\nd\\in[m+1-k,\\,n-1].\n\\]\n\nThe interval \\([m+1-k,\\,n-1]\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nn-(m+1-k)=k\n\\]\n\nintegers; denote this set of integers by  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{\\,d\\mid d=a-b\\text{ for some }(a,b)\\in R\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(d\\in D\\) introduce the line  \n\n\\[\nS_d:\\; y = x-d .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_d\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(S_d\\) is sunny\n(it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_d\\mid d\\in D\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(|D|=k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nNow take any \\((a,b)\\in R\\).  By definition \\(d=a-b\\in D\\), and the equation of \\(S_d\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = a-d .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_d\\).  Consequently every point of \\(R\\) is covered by one of the lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Verification of the whole construction\n\nThe total family of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal V|=m=n-k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\); therefore \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny, while those in \\(\\mathcal V\\) are not; hence exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) it lies on a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal V\\).  \nIf \\(a>m\\) then \\((a,b)\\in R\\) and, as shown in §2.4, it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a-b}\\in\\mathcal S\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll required conditions are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Necessarily \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the construction above).\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
