ine \\(S_{a-m-1}\\).\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal S\\) covers the whole region \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Verification of the whole construction\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal V|=m=n-k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\); therefore \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny, while those in \\(\\mathcal V\\) are\nnot; hence exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) the point lies on \\(V_a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\nIf \\(a>m\\) then \\((a,b)\\in R\\) and, as shown in §2.4, it lies on the\nsunny line \\(S_{a-m-1}\\in\\mathcal S\\).  Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is\ncontained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll required conditions are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (3) together with the explicit construction of\n§2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and having exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
