            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a line is either sunny or belongs to the forbidden family\n\\(\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\).  If we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines obviously satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\},\\qquad |\\mathcal V|=m=n-k .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points already covered\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line\n\\(V_a\\).  Thus every point with abscissa at most \\(m\\) is already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The remaining region\n\nAssume now that \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a>m\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence all points not yet covered belong to  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Sunny lines covering \\(R\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = t\\,x - t\\,(m+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_t\\) equals \\(t\\); therefore each \\(S_t\\) is sunny, and the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) are distinct.\n\n*Coverage of \\(R\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in R\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\na=m+u\\qquad\\text{with }1\\le u\\le k .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(R\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-a = k+1-u .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nChoose the line \\(S_u\\) (its slope is \\(u\\)).  Substituting \\(a=m+u\\) into\n(11) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = u\\,(m+u)-u\\,(m+1)=u .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((m+u,u)\\) lies on \\(S_u\\).  Because the slope of \\(S_u\\) is\n\\(u\\), the set of integer points of \\(S_u\\) whose abscissa is at least\n\\(m+u\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (m+u+j,\\;u+j)\\mid j=0,1,\\dots ,u-1 \\,\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFor a given \\(b\\) satisfying (13) we set \\(j=b-1\\) (note that\n\\(0\\le j\\le u-1\\) because \\(b\\le k+1-u\\le u\\)).  Then the point\n\\((m+u+j,\\;u+j)=(m+u+b-1,\\;b)\\) belongs to \\(S_u\\).  Since \\(a=m+u\\),\nthe equality \\(a=m+u+b-1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); however, the crucial observation\nis that **every** point \\((a,b)\\) of the region \\(R\\) can be written uniquely as\n\\((m+u+b-1,b)\\) with \\(u=a-m\\) and \\(j=b-1\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on\nthe sunny line \\(S_{a-m}\\).\n\nHence each lattice point of \\(R\\) is covered by exactly one of the sunny\nlines \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Verification of the whole construction\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal V|=m=n-k\\) and we have added \\(k\\) sunny lines, so\n\\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) are sunny, while the vertical lines are\nnot; therefore exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) the point lies on the vertical line\n\\(V_a\\in\\mathcal V\\).  If \\(a>m\\) then \\((a,b)\\in R\\) and, as proved in\n§2.4, it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a-m}\\).  Thus every required lattice\npoint belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll conditions of the problem are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (9) together with the explicit construction above shows\nthat for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and\nexactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.  Hence the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
