\) (the only remaining possible value of \\(a\\)), whence \\(b\\le 2\\) and the equality \\(a+b=n+1\\) holds only for \\((a,b)=(n-1,2)\\).  \n     The point \\((n-1,2)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n\n   Thus every required point is covered by at least one of the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Adding sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (for instance, slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\)).  \n   For each chosen slope \\(m\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_m:\\;y=m(x-1)+1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through the already covered point \\((1,1)\\).  \n   Each \\(S_m\\) is sunny by construction, and because it contains \\((1,1)\\) the covering condition is not destroyed.  \n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+k=n+k-1\\;,\n   \\]\n\n   but we need exactly \\(n\\) lines.  To obtain the required number we simply discard any \\(k\\) of the vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,n-2\\) (there are at least \\(k\\) of them because \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \n   After this removal we are left with  \n\n   * \\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines,  \n   * the diagonal line \\(D\\),  \n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_m\\),\n\n   which together constitute exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n   The covering argument above still holds, because every point that lost its vertical line either lies on the diagonal \\(D\\) or on one of the sunny lines (both pass through \\((1,1)\\) and therefore cover all points with \\(a=1\\); points with \\(a>1\\) are still covered by the remaining vertical lines).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible }k\\text{ satisfy }0\\le k\\le n-2}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the