ill cover all points of \\(S_n\\). All \\(\\ell_i\\) have slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\), hence they are sunny, and they are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)** –  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Since there are only three forbidden directions, at most three lines can be non‑sunny.  \n   If exactly three lines are non‑sunny, they must be respectively parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Counting the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on each of these three families shows that together they can cover at most \\((n-1)+(n-1)+(n-1)=3n-3\\) points, whereas \\(|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-3\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Hence three non‑sunny lines are insufficient, and with fewer than three non‑sunny lines the situation is even worse. Consequently a configuration with \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nCombining the construction (existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) with the impossibility proof (non‑existence for \\(k\\ge n-1\\)) yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus a non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_1:\\; \\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\mathcal D_2:\\; \\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\mathcal D_3:\\; \\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nSuppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(s\\) of them are sunny.  \nConsequently \\(n-s\\) lines are non‑sunny, i.e. each belongs to one of the three families \\(\\mathcal D_1,\\mathcal D_2,\\mathcal D_3\\).\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal D_1\\) (horizontal) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) because the largest possible \\(x\\)-coordinate in \\(S_n\\) is \\(n\\).  \nSimilarly each line of \\(\\mathcal D_2\\) (vertical) contains at most \\(n\\) points, and each line of \\(\\mathcal D_3\\) (diagonal) contains at most \\(n\\) points (the points lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\) intersecting the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points).\n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)\\,n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, so  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{that is}\\qquad\nn-s\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nA sharper bound is obtained by observing that the three families are *disjoint*: a line cannot be simultaneously parallel to two different forbidden directions.  \nConsequently at most three of the \\(n\\) lines can be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(n-s\\le 3\\).  \nIf \\(n-s\\le 2\\) then the number of points covered is at most \\(2n<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), a contradiction.  \nHence we must have \\(n-s=3\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\ns=n-3.\n\\]\n\nBut the three non‑sunny lines can be chosen only as one horizontal, one vertical and one diagonal; they together cover at most \\(3n-3\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nthe case \\(n-s=3\\) is also impossible.  \nTherefore the only possible values are  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each such \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1  The base configuration (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L_i:\\; x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\n&M:\\; y=1,\\\\\n&D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal, diagonal respectively).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on \\(M\\)), or \\(a\\le n-2\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\)), or \\(a+b=n+1\\) (hence lies on \\(D\\)).  \nThus (1) covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Replacing vertical lines by sunny ones\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) replace the vertical line \\(L_i\\) in (1) by the line\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,0)\\); the former belongs to \\(S_n\\) (since \\(i+1\\le n\\) and \\(i+1+1\\le n+1\\)), while the latter is not required to be covered.  \nConsequently the replacement does not destroy the covering property: the point \\((i,1)\\) that was previously on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) is still covered, and all other points of \\(S_n\\) remain on the unchanged lines.\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\), the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.  \n\nThe resulting family consists of\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\);\n* the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=i\\) with \\(k+1\\le i\\le n-2\\);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nAltogether there are \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration with at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nThen at most two lines are non‑sunny, i.e. they belong to the families \\(\\mathcal D_1,\\mathcal D_2,\\mathcal D_3\\).\n\n*If there is only one non‑sunny line*, it can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\), far fewer than \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*If there are exactly two non‑sunny lines*, the maximum number of points they can jointly cover is \\(2n\\) (each line contributes at most \\(n\\) points).  \nAgain \\(2n<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence at least three non‑sunny lines would be necessary, contradicting the assumption that at most two are non‑sunny.  \nTherefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) we have exhibited an explicit construction, and we have proved that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ are possible, and no other }k\\text{ works}.}\n\\]"
