e replacement for each yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=1\\) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and all \\(k\\ge 2\\) gives the full answer.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that exactly one of them, say \\(L_{1}\\), is sunny.  \nAll other lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; we partition them as  \n\n* \\(\\mathcal H\\): horizontal lines (slope \\(0\\));\n* \\(\\mathcal V\\): vertical lines (slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* \\(\\mathcal D\\): lines of slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\qquad v=|\\mathcal V|,\\qquad d=|\\mathcal D|,\n\\]\nso that \\(h+v+d=n-1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the **layer**\n\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{s}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe layers are pairwise disjoint and \\(|\\Lambda_{s}|=s-1\\).\n\n* A horizontal line has a fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate, therefore it can intersect at most one point of each \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\).  \n* A vertical line has a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate, likewise at most one point of each \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\).  \n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\); it can intersect at most one point of each \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\) (indeed it meets \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\) only when \\(c=s\\)).\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can be covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(h+v+d)(n-1)=(n-1)^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(|T_{n}|\\) is the sum of the sizes of the layers:\n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)^{2}= \\frac{-n^{2}+5n-2}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nso (1) does not suffice to cover all points; at least one point of \\(T_{n}\\) is not on any non‑sunny line.  \nThat uncovered point must lie on the unique sunny line \\(L_{1}\\).\n\nA sunny line, having a slope different from \\(-1\\), meets each layer \\(\\Lambda_{s}\\) in **at most one** point (otherwise two points of the same layer would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) layers, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nTherefore the whole family can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)^{2}+n = n^{2}-n+1 \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nBut from (2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n^{2}-n+1)=\\frac{-n^{2}+3n-2}{2}>0\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nso \\(|T_{n}|>n^{2}-n+1\\).  \nThus (3) is insufficient, a contradiction.  \nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) take the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; x+y=t .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none is sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) such lines.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the covering condition holds and we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n##### 2.2  Replacing a line by a sunny one  \n\nFix a particular integer \\(t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\)).  \nConsider the two extreme lattice points of the layer \\(\\Lambda_{t}\\),\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,t-1),\\qquad P_{2}=(t-1,1).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL^{\\ast}_{t}:\\;(t-2)x+(t-2)y=2(t-1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{t-2}{2-t}= -1+\\frac{2}{2-t},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(t\\ge 2\\); therefore \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) is sunny.\n\nThe integer points of \\(\\Lambda_{t}\\) are precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le t-1\\).  \nAll of them lie on the segment joining \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\), hence belong to the line \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\).  \nConsequently replacing the original line \\(x+y=t\\) by \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) does **not** change the set of covered points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n##### 2.3  Obtaining any \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\) (\\(k\\ge 2\\)).  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) replace the line \\(x+y=t\\) by the sunny line \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) described above; for the remaining values of \\(t\\) keep the original line \\(x+y=t\\).\n\nThe resulting family consists of  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L^{\\ast}_{t}\\mid t\\in S\\}\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(\\{x+y=t\\mid t\\notin S\\}\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because the slopes of the \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) are all different from each other and from \\(-1\\).  \nEach layer \\(\\Lambda_{t}\\) is still covered (by \\(L^{\\ast}_{t}\\) if \\(t\\in S\\), otherwise by \\(x+y=t\\)).  \nHence the covering condition is satisfied and we have a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\), together with the case \\(k=0\\) we have exhibited configurations for all admissible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\n* No configuration can have exactly one sunny line (Section 1).  \n* Configurations exist for \\(k=0\\) (Section 2.1) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) (Section 2.3).\n\nTherefore the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
