d b\\ge 1\\}\\) contains the points  \n     \\[\n     (a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a).\n     \\]\n     The topmost point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) lies on the diagonal, the bottom point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), and the intermediate points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-a\\) belong to \\(M\\).\n\n   \u2013 A **vertical** line \\(x=a\\) (which is non\u2011sunny) covers *all* points of the column, i.e. it covers the diagonal point, the bottom point and every intermediate point.  No sunny line can do this, because a non\u2011vertical line meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.\n\n   \u2013 Therefore, for each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) we must have a vertical line \\(x=a\\) in the family; otherwise the intermediate points of that column would remain uncovered.\n\n   \u2013 The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (also non\u2011sunny) covers the single point \\((n,1)\\) and, together with the vertical lines, also covers the bottom points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(a\\le n-2\\).\n\n   \u2013 Altogether we obtain the following forced set of lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\;L_0:\\;x+y=n+1\\;}\\qquad\n     \\boxed{\\;L_h:\\;y=1\\;}\\qquad\n     \\boxed{\\;L_a:\\;x=a\\;(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-2)\\;}.\n     \\]\n     These are exactly \\(1+(1)+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines, and they cover every point of \\(P_n\\).\n\n   \u2013 Since every line in any admissible configuration must belong to the forced set above, **no sunny line can appear**.  Hence any admissible configuration contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines, and consequently \\(k\\le 0\\).\n\n2. **Sufficiency.**  \n   The family displayed in the previous paragraph indeed satisfies both requirements:\n   * it consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines;\n   * each line is non\u2011sunny (vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\));\n   * for every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have:\n     - if \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(L_0\\);\n     - if \\(b=1\\) the point lies 